@dougwilson : ok, added everything. It is rebased, added content-length checks (see if you like them, I'm not 100% sure!), and also code to detect pre-set encoding. The caveat is that this final thing fails the test in node 0.8.

Note also that I delete req.headers['content-length'] if compressed, for the sake of downstream middlewares, which I didn't do previously.

If you like everything, just let me know how to fix the test. If there's a Travis directive I can use, or simply use runtime logic in the test to detect streams2+ and only then run the test?

@dougwilson : ok, works in 0.8 as well, thanks for the tip.

As for `content-length` -- I suppose it can't hurt. While it's true that there will be nothing to consume, I wouldn't want downstream middleware to look at the headers and try to guess something about the content give its "length".

If you still feel that way -- by all means I'll remove.

I just found out thanks to node.js > 0.8 but < 0.10.6, we need this conditional to be:

``` js
var state = req._readableState;
if (req._decoder || (state && (state.encoding || state.decoder))) {
```

otherwise people using older versions of 0.10 will always trigger this error (since `req._readableState.encoding` will be `undefined`).
